; xfail: wrong number of arguments for Two: got 3, expected 2
;
; ONE argument too many on a data-class construction (2026-08-18). The
; opposite direction has been a hard diagnostic for a while ("property c
; would be left uninitialized"); this one compiled with rc 0 and answered
; an UNDEFINED value — `Two(1, 2, 3)` printed 5, as though the FIRST
; argument had fallen away, because Emitter.emit_tuple's field-store loop is
; bounded by the ARGUMENT count while LLVMStructGetTypeAtIndex indexes over
; FIELDS: past the last field the query is out of range (its only guard in
; LLVM is an assert, compiled out of a release libLLVM) and the bytes that
; come back are dereferenced as a Type*. The 5 is NOT a specification and
; must never be asserted — this test pins the DIAGNOSIS.

define Two (a: int  b: int)

let x Two(1, 2, 3)
print("compiled\n")
